  {
    'subject': 'Physics',
    'chapterTitle': 'Physical Quantities and Units',
    'body': 'Physics is the branch of science that studies matter, energy and their interactions. Every measurement in physics is a physical quantity, which consists of a numerical value and a unit. Physical quantities are divided into fundamental (base) quantities and derived quantities. There are seven fundamental quantities: length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), electric current (ampere, A), temperature (kelvin, K), amount of substance (mole, mol) and luminous intensity (candela, cd). Derived quantities are obtained by combining fundamental quantities through multiplication or division, for example area (m^2), volume (m^3), speed (m/s), density (kg/m^3) and force (kg m/s^2, called the newton).\n\nThe internationally accepted system of units is the Systeme International (SI), which ensures uniformity in scientific measurement and communication worldwide. Prefixes are used with SI units to express very large or very small quantities conveniently: kilo (10^3), centi (10^-2), milli (10^-3), micro (10^-6), nano (10^-9) and mega (10^6) are the most commonly used in WAEC/WASSCE physics.\n\nDimensional analysis is a technique that uses the fundamental quantities (mass M, length L, time T) to check the correctness of physical equations and to derive relationships between quantities. For example, the dimension of speed is [L][T]^-1, and the dimension of force is [M][L][T]^-2. If both sides of an equation do not have the same dimensions, the equation is incorrect. This method is very useful in verifying formulae before using them in calculations.\n\nMeasurement of physical quantities requires appropriate instruments: length is measured with metre rules, vernier calipers (for small lengths with precision to 0.01 cm) and micrometer screw gauges (precision to 0.001 cm); mass is measured with a beam balance or triple beam balance; time is measured with stopwatches or stopclocks. Every measuring instrument has a limit of accuracy called its least count, and every measurement carries an inherent uncertainty called experimental error.\n\nErrors in measurement may be systematic, arising from a fault in the instrument such as zero error, or random, arising from unpredictable variations during repeated readings. Zero error occurs when an instrument does not read zero when it should, and it must be added to or subtracted from every subsequent reading depending on whether it is positive or negative. Good experimental practice minimizes error by taking multiple readings and calculating an average, by avoiding parallax error when reading a scale, and by ensuring the eye is positioned perpendicular to the scale being read.\n\nSample Exam-Style Questions:\n1. Question: State the seven fundamental quantities and their SI units. Answer: Length (metre), mass (kilogram), time (second), electric current (ampere), temperature (kelvin), amount of substance (mole), luminous intensity (candela).\n2. Question: Show that the equation v = u + at is dimensionally consistent. Answer: v and u have dimension [L][T]^-1. at has dimension [L][T]^-2 multiplied by [T] which gives [L][T]^-1. Since all terms have the same dimension, the equation is dimensionally consistent.\n3. Question: Oyedele used a micrometer screw gauge to measure the diameter of a wire and obtained a reading of 0.52 mm, but the gauge had a zero error of +0.02 mm. Find the true diameter. Answer: True diameter equals observed reading minus zero error, that is 0.52 mm minus 0.02 mm, which equals 0.50 mm.\n4. Question: Convert 5 kilometres to metres and 250 milligrams to kilograms. Answer: 5 km equals 5 multiplied by 1000, which equals 5000 m. 250 mg equals 250 divided by 1,000,000, which equals 0.00025 kg.\n\nRevision and Summary Points:\n- Physics studies matter, energy and their interactions.\n- A physical quantity has a numerical value and a unit.\n- Fundamental quantities are length, mass, time, current, temperature, amount of substance and luminous intensity.\n- Derived quantities are combinations of fundamental quantities.\n- SI stands for Systeme International, the standard system of units.\n- The SI unit of length is the metre, of mass the kilogram, of time the second.\n- Prefixes like kilo, centi, milli, micro and nano scale units up or down.\n- Dimensional analysis checks the correctness of equations.\n- Dimensions are expressed in terms of M, L and T.\n- Both sides of a correct equation must have the same dimensions.\n- Vernier calipers measure small lengths to 0.01 cm precision.\n- Micrometer screw gauges measure to 0.001 cm precision.\n- A beam balance measures mass by comparison.\n- Stopwatches and stopclocks measure time intervals.\n- Least count is the smallest measurement an instrument can accurately give.\n- Systematic errors arise from instrument faults such as zero error.\n- Random errors arise from unpredictable variations in readings.\n- Zero error must be corrected by adding or subtracting from readings.\n- Parallax error occurs when the eye is not perpendicular to the scale.\n- Taking an average of several readings reduces random error.\n- Precision refers to consistency of repeated measurements.\n- Accuracy refers to closeness of a measurement to the true value.\n- Significant figures indicate the precision of a recorded value.\n- SI base units form the foundation for all other units in physics.\n- Volume is a derived quantity with unit m^3.\n- Density is mass per unit volume, a derived quantity.\n- Speed is distance covered per unit time.\n- Force has the derived unit newton, equal to kg m/s^2.\n- Conversion between units requires correct powers of ten.\n- Good measurement technique reduces both systematic and random error.',
